{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aed0417c000060ee","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014: Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n10\t\n11\t## Triage\n12\t\n13\tNot below-threshold: the strategy spans three components (Llama Stack, TrustyAI,\n14\tDashboard) and three owning teams (RHOAI GenAI, TrustyAI, RHOAI Dashboard),\n15\tand the scope mixes investigation, code hardening, integration promotion, and\n16\tdocumentation — architecturally distinct work streams. Not docs-only: Llama\n17\tStack and Dashboard require code changes. Full decomposition follows.\n18\t\n19\t## Step 1 — Strategy Structure Notes\n20\t\n21\tThe strategy provides Problem Statement, HLRs (P0/P1/P2), Affected Components,\n22\tOut of Scope, and Open Questions. **Missing sections** (health warning): no\n23\tImpacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance\n24\tCriteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were taken from the\n25\tarchitecture-context files (Llama Stack → RHOAI GenAI, TrustyAI → TrustyAI,\n26\tDashboard → RHOAI Dashboard). Epic acceptance criteria were derived from the\n27\tHLR text. No Staff Engineer Input section is present.\n28\t\n29\t## Component Graph\n30\t\n31\tAll three affected components exist in `.context/architecture-context/` — no\n32\tnew-component onboarding chain is needed, and all are Konflux-onboarded.\n33\t\n34\t- **Llama Stack** (RHOAI GenAI) — active. Architecture context confirms\n35\t  maturity **Tech Preview**, motivating the GA program. Carries the bulk of\n36\t  the work: assessment, API stability, gap remediation, support posture, and\n37\t  the TrustyAI guardrails integration promotion.\n38\t- **TrustyAI** (TrustyAI team) — **passive**. Architecture context confirms\n39\t  TrustyAI is already Stable (GA) and ships the guardrails services; the\n40\t  strategy's change (promote the Llama Stack distribution's experimental\n41\t  guardrails flag to a supported integration) is made on the Llama Stack side.\n42\t  No TrustyAI code changes are stated. Per the passive-component rule,\n43\t  validation of the integration becomes acceptance criteria on the nearest\n44\t  active component's epic (the guardrails promotion epic), which includes\n45\t  joint validation with the TrustyAI service. Mapping note: that epic's\n46\t  `component` is `Llama Stack` (the most-relevant canonical name) even though\n47\t  the strategy lists both Llama Stack and TrustyAI as affected.\n48\t- **Dashboard** (RHOAI Dashboard) — active. Default enablement of Llama Stack\n49\t  management for entitled clusters (removing the TP opt-in gate) is Dashboard\n50\t  code, driven by its per-component feature flags.\n51\t\n52\t## Investigation Analysis\n53\t\n54\tThe strategy's single open question — the GA scope is unknown until a\n55\tsystematic GA-readiness assessment lands (TP feedback scattered across support\n56\ttickets; no assessment done) — is a **genuine unknown whose answer changes\n57\twhat downstream work does**. It maps directly to HLR-1 and becomes the\n58\tInvestigation epic. The outcome space is **unbounded** (an inventory of\n59\tunknown gaps cannot be enumerated into ≤3 branches), so this is a **phased\n60\tdecomposition**, not a conditional-branch decomposition: the gap-remediation\n61\tImplementation epic is gated on the assessment's findings and re-baselined\n62\tagainst its output. The explicitly enumerated HLR-2 (API stability) and HLR-3\n63\t(support posture) deliverables do not depend on the assessment for their\n64\texistence or shape and proceed in parallel.\n65\t\n66\t## Epic List\n67\t\n68\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n69\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n70\t| E001 | Llama Stack GA-readiness gap assessment | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n71\t| E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantees and tested TP upgrade path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n72\t| E003 | Remediate Llama Stack GA-readiness gaps (scale, error handling, assessment findings) | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n73\t| E004 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n74\t| E005 | Promote TrustyAI guardrails to a supported integration in Llama Stack distributions | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P1 |\n75\t| E006 | Enable Llama Stack management by default in the Dashboard for entitled clusters | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n76\t| E007 | GA documentation uplift and TP migration notes for Llama Stack | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P0 |\n77\t\n78\tPriorities: E002/E004 map to P0 HLRs directly. E003 maps to P0 HLR-1 (closure\n79\tof the assessed gaps) and blocks the P0 graduation, hence P0 by priority\n80\tinheritance. E005/E006 map to P1 HLRs. E007 is a `docs-authoring` epic: by the\n81\tdocs-authoring priority exception its priority derives from the strategy\n82\tfrontmatter (`priority: Critical`) → P0, and it does not push priority\n83\tupstream.\n84\t\n85\t## Dependency DAG\n86\t\n87\t```mermaid\n88\tgraph TD\n89\t    E001[E001 GA-readiness assessment] --> E003[E003 Gap remediation]\n90\t    E002[E002 API stability] --> E007[E007 GA docs]\n91\t    E003 --> E007\n92\t    E004[E004 Support posture] --> E007\n93\t    E005[E005 TrustyAI guardrails supported] --> E007\n94\t    E006[E006 Dashboard default enablement] --> E007\n95\t```\n96\t\n97\t## DAG Justification\n98\t\n99\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n100\t|------|------|-----------|\n101\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The assessment's findings define the gap-remediation epic's scope; true gate with `rewrite` impact (scope/approach re-baselined on findings). Unbounded outcome → phased decomposition, no branch files. |\n102\t| E001 ∥ E002, E004, E005, E006 | Rule 14 / Step 3 decision rule | HLR-2/HLR-3 deliverables are explicitly enumerated and proceed the same way regardless of assessment findings; the Dashboard and guardrails work share no artifacts with the assessment. No edge. |\n103\t| E002 ∥ E004 ∥ E005 | Rule 14/15 | Same component and team but distinct work surfaces (API surface vs. support-process artifacts vs. integration promotion); no shared data structure or API endpoint is built by two epics. Parallel. |\n104\t| E006 ∥ all Llama Stack epics | Rule 14 | Different repo (odh-dashboard), no shared build artifacts — the Dashboard works through Kubernetes CRs, not Llama Stack code. Parallel. |\n105\t| E002..E006 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs last) | `docs-authoring` is blocked by every Implementation epic; docs describe what was built, including the remediated gaps, support posture, guardrails integration, and default enablement. |\n106\t\n107\tNo `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics are\n108\tgenerated: all components are in the platform inventory and already Konflux\n109\tonboarded per architecture context.\n110\t\n111\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n112\t\n113\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n114\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n115\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 (assessment), E003 (gap closure) | The HLR's deliverable is the assessment producing the definitive GA scope (E001); closing the inventoried gaps is the follow-on work (E003), gated on E001's output. |\n116\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | Full coverage: versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path from last two TP releases. |\n117\t| HLR-3 | P0 | E004 | SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix. |\n118\t| HLR-4 | P1 | E005 | TrustyAI is a passive component (no code changes on its side); integration validation is an AC on E005. |\n119\t| HLR-5 | P1 | E006 | Dashboard-owned; feature-flag/default change. |\n120\t| HLR-6 | P2 | E007 | `bundled`? No — documentation is a distinct work-product stream and gets its own `docs-authoring` epic per Rule 11; its P0 priority comes from the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy `priority: Critical`), not from HLR-6's P2 label. |\n121\t\n122\tAll six HLRs are covered; none orphaned.\n123\t\n124\t## Health Warnings\n125\t\n126\t- **Missing strategy sections**: no Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table,\n127\t  Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, or NFRs. Teams inferred from\n128\t  architecture context; epic ACs derived from HLR text. Human verification of\n129\t  team assignments recommended.\n130\t- **Scope uncertainty on gap remediation**: until the GA-readiness assessment\n131\t  lands, the size of the remediation work is an estimate (the strategy's open\n132\t  question). The remediation epic may land above the ~2-week epic guideline\n133\t  once the assessment's gap inventory is known; re-split it along gap-category\n134\t  lines (e.g. API stability / scale limits / error handling) if the assessment\n135\t  inventories more than three distinct remediation streams or any single\n136\t  category estimated beyond one sprint.\n137\t- **Priority label vs. docs exception**: the documentation epic is P0 by the\n138\t  docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Critical) even though\n139\t  its HLR is P2. This is rule-mandated, not a priority inversion in the\n140\t  strategy; planners should treat the docs epic's scheduling as\n141\t  last-in-chain anyway (Rule 11).\n142\t- **Release coordination, not a DAG edge**: default-enabling Llama Stack\n143\t  management in the Dashboard (entitled clusters) should be coordinated with\n144\t  the GA graduation timing, but the Dashboard work builds against existing\n145\t  Kubernetes CRs and shares no build artifact with the Llama Stack epics —\n146\t  the coupling is release timing, expressed here as a note, not a dependency.\n147\t- **TrustyAI cross-team validation**: the guardrails promotion epic is owned\n148\t  by RHOAI GenAI; its acceptance criteria require joint validation of the\n149\t  integration with the TrustyAI service and TrustyAI-team sign-off that the\n150\t  integration is supportable, since TrustyAI is owned by a different team. If\n151\t  TrustyAI-side code changes turn out to be needed, that becomes separate\n152\t  scope for the TrustyAI team.\n153\t\n154\t## Tiered Delivery\n155\t\n156\tNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing was identified\n157\t(no Dependencies table in the strategy; all components are in-platform). The\n158\tassessment-gated remediation epic is phased decomposition, not tiered\n159\tdelivery.\n160\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b7df2bc2f05c9eb","agent_id":"a0d42e19ed088d994","session_id":"a5ccff59-e83b-4c36-b1be-14c65ff6e583","uuid":"6aa65d57-e259-41de-b07b-7a60851026fe","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:55:45.645Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-aed0417c000060ee","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
