rustyAI guardrails integration and the\n53\t  default-enabled Dashboard management behavior as part of the GA\n54\t  documentation set.\n55\t- Content outline (topics to be authored or rewritten):\n56\t  - GA readiness and support posture overview (SLOs, backport policy,\n57\t    supported configuration matrix, as published by the support-posture work)\n58\t  - Upgrade and migration from TP releases (migration notes, deprecated\n59\t    surfaces, upgrade path)\n60\t  - Supported TrustyAI guardrails integration usage\n61\t  - Dashboard management of Llama Stack distributions (default-enabled for\n62\t    entitled clusters)\n63\t  - Any gap-remediation behavior changes surfaced to users (scale limits,\n64\t    error handling) identified by the GA-readiness assessment's remediation\n65\t\n66\t## Acceptance Criteria\n67\t\n68\t- Llama Stack documentation meets GA standards as accepted by the docs\n69\t  review: no TP-era caveats left on supported surfaces, supported usage is\n70\t  accurately described.\n71\t- Migration notes from TP exist and cover the differences between the last\n72\t  two TP releases and GA, including deprecated API surfaces per the\n73\t  deprecation policy.\n74\t- Documentation for the supported TrustyAI guardrails integration and\n75\t  default-enabled Dashboard management is published.\n76\t- All content is technically reviewed against the implemented GA behavior\n77\t  (API stability work, support posture, guardrails integration, Dashboard\n78\t  enablement, gap remediation) by the owning engineers, with review sign-off\n79\t  recorded.\n80\t\n81\t## HLR Traceability\n82\t\n83\t- **HLR-6 (P2)**: Documentation is uplifted to GA standards, including\n84\t  migration notes from TP. Full coverage of this HLR.\n85\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-963f9fd92f5abe9a","agent_id":"a8de30c105ee98776","session_id":"a5ccff59-e83b-4c36-b1be-14c65ff6e583","uuid":"b9c91c41-db52-40f1-aa46-7cebad5472d6","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:56:49.454Z","tool_resul